Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) might be giants if distant, but apparent magnitude alone is not a reliable indicator of giant status because it depends on distance.
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means greater distance. Giants tend to be luminous and often farther away, so smaller Plx may correlate with giants.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces reliability of distance estimate, so less useful directly.
- B-V (color index): Giants have characteristic color indices depending on spectral type; generally, redder (higher B-V) stars can be giants, but color alone is not definitive.
- SpType (spectral type): Spectral types with luminosity classes III, II, Ib, etc. indicate giant or supergiant stars, while V indicates main sequence (dwarfs).
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower absolute magnitude (brighter) than dwarfs; smaller Amag values (more negative or lower numerically) indicate giants.

In summary, spectral type luminosity class and absolute magnitude are the strongest indicators of giant status, with parallax and color index providing supporting evidence.

